1. What is Data & Database?
What is Data?

Data is a collection of raw facts and information.

Examples:

Ankita
25
Pune
B.Sc. Computer Science
85

These individual values are called data.

What is a Database?

A database is an organized collection of data that allows us to store, manage, and retrieve information easily.

Real-world example

A college may need to store:

Student ID
Student Name
Age
Course
Marks
Mobile Number

Instead of maintaining thousands of records in notebooks, we can store them in a database.

Simple example
Student Database
│
├── Student ID
├── Name
├── Age
├── Course
└── Marks

Simple definition for students:

A database is an organized collection of related data.

2. What is DBMS?

DBMS = Database Management System

A DBMS is software used to create, store, manage, update, and retrieve data from a database.

Examples of DBMS
MySQL
Oracle Database
Microsoft SQL Server
PostgreSQL
SQLite
What can a DBMS do?

A DBMS allows us to:

Create databases
Create tables
Insert data
Update data
Delete data
Search/retrieve data
Control access to data
Example

Suppose a college has 10,000 student records.

Using a DBMS, we can easily ask:

SELECT * FROM students;

and retrieve the student information.

Simple definition

DBMS is software that helps us manage databases.

3. DBMS vs RDBMS
DBMS

DBMS = Database Management System

It is a system used to manage data.

RDBMS

RDBMS = Relational Database Management System

It stores data in tables and establishes relationships between tables.

Example

Suppose we have:

Students table

student_id	name	course_id
1	Rahul	101
2	Priya	102

Courses table

course_id	course_name
101	BCA
102	BSc CS

Here, course_id connects the two tables.

This is the relational concept.

DBMS vs RDBMS
DBMS	RDBMS
Database Management System	Relational Database Management System
May not use relationships between tables	Uses relationships between tables
Data organization can vary	Data is generally organized into tables
Relationship support is limited/not inherent	Supports relationships using keys
Suitable for simpler data management	Suitable for relational applications
Examples

RDBMS:

MySQL
Oracle
PostgreSQL
SQL Server

Important: MySQL is an RDBMS.

4. Introduction to SQL

SQL = Structured Query Language

SQL is used to communicate with and manage data in relational databases.

We can use SQL to:

Create databases
Create tables
Insert data
Retrieve data
Update data
Delete data
Example
SELECT * FROM students;

This SQL query means:

Display all records from the students table.

Another example
INSERT INTO students VALUES
(1, 'Rahul', 20, 'BCA');

This adds a student record.

Simple definition

SQL is a language used to communicate with relational databases.

6. Introduction to MySQL

MySQL is an open-source relational database management system (RDBMS) that uses SQL to work with relational data.

For example, a Java/Spring Boot application can use MySQL to store:

Users
Products
Orders
Payments
Students
Employees
Example architecture
Java / Spring Boot Application
            ↓
           SQL
            ↓
          MySQL
            ↓
        Database
            ↓
          Tables
SQL vs MySQL

This is an important distinction for beginners:

SQL → Language

MySQL → Database Management System that uses SQL

For example:

SELECT * FROM students;

is SQL.

MySQL is one of the database systems in which you can execute that SQL.

7. Creating a Database

We can create a database using CREATE DATABASE.

Syntax
CREATE DATABASE database_name;
Example
CREATE DATABASE college;

Now we have a database called college.

Check available databases
SHOW DATABASES;
Select the database

Before creating tables, select the database:

USE college;

Now any tables we create will be created inside the college database.

Complete example
CREATE DATABASE college;

USE college;
8. Creating Tables

A table stores data in the form of rows and columns.

For example:

Students
id	name	age	course
1	Rahul	20	BCA
2	Priya	21	BSc CS
Create the table
CREATE TABLE students (
    id INT,
    name VARCHAR(50),
    age INT,
    course VARCHAR(30)
);
Check tables
SHOW TABLES;
See table structure
DESC students;

or:

DESCRIBE students;
9. Understanding Data Types

A data type defines what kind of value can be stored in a column.

Some commonly used MySQL data types are:

Data Type	Used For	Example
INT	Whole numbers	25
DECIMAL	Decimal numbers	95.50
VARCHAR	Variable-length text	'Rahul'
CHAR	Fixed-length text	'M'
DATE	Date	'2026-09-20'
TIME	Time	'10:30:00'
DATETIME	Date + time	'2026-09-20 10:30:00'
TEXT	Large text	'Description...'
BOOLEAN	True/false style values	TRUE
Example
CREATE TABLE students (
    id INT,
    name VARCHAR(50),
    age INT,
    marks DECIMAL(5,2),
    birth_date DATE
);

Here:

id          → INT
name        → VARCHAR
age         → INT
marks       → DECIMAL
birth_date  → DATE
VARCHAR(50)
name VARCHAR(50)

means the column can store variable-length text up to 50 characters.

10. INSERT — Adding Records

INSERT is used to add records/rows into a table.

Suppose we have:

CREATE TABLE students (
    id INT,
    name VARCHAR(50),
    age INT,
    course VARCHAR(30)
);
Method 1: INSERT with column names
INSERT INTO students (id, name, age, course)
VALUES (1, 'Rahul', 20, 'BCA');

Insert another student:

INSERT INTO students (id, name, age, course)
VALUES (2, 'Priya', 21, 'BSc CS');
INSERT Multiple Records

You can insert multiple rows using a single query:

INSERT INTO students (id, name, age, course)
VALUES
(3, 'Amit', 19, 'BCA'),
(4, 'Sneha', 22, 'BSc CS'),
(5, 'Rohit', 20, 'BCA');

This is very useful when inserting multiple records.

Method 2: INSERT without column names

If you provide values for all columns in the exact table-column order, you can omit the column names:

INSERT INTO students
VALUES (6, 'Neha', 21, 'BSc CS');
Multiple records without column names
INSERT INTO students
VALUES
(7, 'Kiran', 20, 'BCA'),
(8, 'Pooja', 21, 'BSc CS'),
(9, 'Akash', 19, 'BCA');
⚠️ Important

When column names are omitted:

INSERT INTO students
VALUES (...);

the values must match the table's column order and compatible data types.

